{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 63, :comments ["<b>And Isaac went out to meditate in the field toward evening.</b> Our Sages, blessed be their memory (Berakhot 26b), learned from here that Isaac established the prayer of Minhah, which is toward evening, close to nightfall. And from here is support for what our Sages, blessed be their memory, said (Berakhot 6b): a person should always be careful with the Minhah prayer, for so Elijah was answered only in the Minhah prayer. And even though Abraham and Jacob also established the prayers of Shaḥarit and Ma'ariv, nevertheless we do not find that they were answered immediately and forthwith; but in the prayer of Minhah we find that Isaac was answered immediately. For presumably Isaac prayed for a match while Eliezer was still on the way, as it says: ״עַל זֹאת יִתְפַּלֵּל כָּל חָסִיד לְעֵת מְצֹא״ — 'this' means a wife (Berakhot 8a). And therefore he went to pray this prayer in the field: if HaShem gave him the field ready for sowing with surpassing excellence above all other fields, as it is written (Genesis 26:12) ״וַיִּזְרַע יִצְחָק בָּאָרֶץ הַהִוא וַיִּמְצָא מֵאָה שְׁעָרִים״, then by a fortiori reasoning He would give him a wife, an everlasting possession, who would bring forth good and upright seed in the eyes of Elohim. And immediately, when he prayed for it, it is written: ״וַיִּשָּׂא עֵינָיו וַיַּרְא וְהִנֵּה גְמַלִּים בָּאִים״, for during the time of prayer he would cast his eyes downward, and immediately upon completing the prayer he lifted his eyes upward and saw, for he was answered immediately — and behold, camels were coming, bearing his mate. And from this they learned that a person is answered more in the prayer of Minhah. And the reason for this: because the attribute of Judgment prosecutes specifically at night, and therefore the prayer of Ma'arit is close to the night, and likewise the prayer of Shaḥarit is also close to the night that has passed; but Minhah is distant from both nights — therefore in this prayer there is no prosecutor at all." "Another reason. This prayer was close to nightfall because before the sun of Sarah had set, the sun of Rebecca had already risen; therefore Scripture informs us that Rebecca arrived close to nightfall, to make known that before the evening [of Sarah's passing] the sun of Rebecca had already risen — so that the world would not lack these righteous women, who are to the world like the orbit of the sun; and so that there would not be lacking a lamp burning in the tent, and a cloud bound over it, and the blessing of the dough — and it is easy to understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:52.023061314Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}